\section*{Additive indecomposability of finite modifications of the primes}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 013; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that a set that agrees with the primes apart from finitely many elements cannot be a sumset of two nonnegative-integer sets each having at least two members.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
Let $\mathcal P$ be the set of primes, and let $A,B\subseteq\mathbb Z_{\ge0}$ with $|A|,|B|\ge2$. For their ordinary sumset $A+B=\{a+b:a\in A,b\in B\}$, the manuscript claims
\[
 |(A+B)\mathbin{\triangle}\mathcal P|=\infty.
\]
Consequently even a finite modification of the prime set cannot be expressed as a sum of two nontrivial sets of nonnegative integers; the symmetric difference condition allows finitely many additions or deletions of primes.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 013; 2026-09-24), finite summands are eliminated by shifted-prime sieving. For two infinite summands, complementary residue supports produce character restrictions at separated prime scales; a transfer comparison contracts their possible coverage until it conflicts with coverage of all sufficiently large primes.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 013; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 013; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: a set that agrees with the primes apart from finitely many elements cannot be a sumset of two nonnegative-integer sets each having at least two members.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{OstmannComplete.lean}, \texttt{OstmannPrimes.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.Ostmann.inverseGoldbach}, \texttt{OAI.Ostmann.main}, \texttt{OAI.Ostmann.twoInfiniteSummandsImpossible}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

